Yes. The SDLC currently has the roadmap defined twice:

1. Version Roadmap — defines V1, V2, V3…


2. SDLC Applied to Each Version — repeats the same version progression.



The clean solution is to keep one authoritative roadmap and make the SDLC section explain how each version passes through the SDLC, without redefining the versions.

Resolved structure

## 22. Version Roadmap

The project evolves incrementally. Each version must solve a real problem and
must pass through the SDLC before being considered complete.

| Version | Scope | Goal |
|---|---|---|
| V1 | Chama Foundation | Chamas, members, memberships, roles, registration fees, contributions, shares |
| V2 | Financial Management | Loans, loan repayments, payouts, ledger |
| V3 | Payment Architecture | Payment domain, attempts, provider transactions, callbacks, provider abstraction |
| V4 | Jenga + M-Pesa | M-Pesa STK payments through Jenga |
| V5 | Payment Reliability | Webhooks, reconciliation, idempotency, transaction verification |
| V6 | KCB BUNI | KCB BUNI payment integration |
| V7 | NCBA | NCBA payment and collection integration |
| V8 | Daraja | Direct Safaricom M-Pesa integration where required |
| V9 | Bank Reconciliation | Bank transactions, reconciliation records, account synchronization |
| V10 | Reporting & Audit | Statements, reports, audit trails |
| V11 | Notifications | SMS, email, payment and financial notifications |
| V12 | Web Application | React web application |
| V13 | Mobile Application | React Native application |
| V14 | USSD | USSD access for core member operations |
| V15 | Production Hardening | Security, performance, observability, backups, recovery and operational improvements |
| V16+ | Product Evolution | Features driven by real users and validated requirements |

### Versioning Rule

A version is not considered complete merely because its code has been written.

Each version must:

1. Define the problem being solved.
2. Define functional and non-functional requirements.
3. Analyze the affected business/domain processes.
4. Update the domain and database model where necessary.
5. Define or update API contracts.
6. Implement the required functionality.
7. Add automated tests.
8. Validate financial correctness where applicable.
9. Apply security and authorization requirements.
10. Deploy to the appropriate environment.
11. Monitor the implementation.
12. Collect feedback.
13. Fix discovered defects before moving to the next version.

